Run `date -u` first and treat that instant as your start; your deadline is 60 minutes later. Read sections 2 and 3 with read_plan(view='section') before starting; they are the authority. This node does not change reckon/hooks/coordinator_obligations.py. A later node switches the hook to your reader. reckon/crew/obligation_snapshot.py holds:
- the atomic writer (a temporary file renamed over the old one);
- the reader;
- the freshness check from section 3: a process with the recorded pid is alive with the recorded start time; the code stamp equals the stamp computed for the main checkout's source; computed_at is within 120 s. It returns fresh, or one of the three not-fresh reasons as a fixed word.
The module imports nothing from reckon._plan_html, reckon._backends or reckon.ledger at module level, because the prompt hook will import it; the writer may import the obligations derivation lazily inside its function. The producer in runs.watch calls the writer on the three triggers section 2 names. Take the producer's pid and start time from the lease registration (runs.read_watch_registration, landed in merge 320061aff). Record the code stamp there through runs.update_watch_registration if it is absent. Producer identity is kept nowhere else.

In a new tests/test_obligations_snapshot_is_published.py, built on a synthesised fleet under the test's temporary path and a temporary configuration home (never the real ones), with sweeps driven explicitly:
- a pointer transition appears in the session's snapshot by the end of the first sweep after it;
- the snapshot equals obligations() over the same unmodified files (the parity case);
- a writer killed between the temporary write and the rename leaves the previous snapshot intact and readable;
- each freshness outcome is returned for its case, including a pid reused by another process;
- importing reckon.crew.obligation_snapshot in a fresh subprocess leaves none of the three derivation modules in sys.modules.
Execute the named negative control and confirm the import case fails under it.

The gate is the new test plus every existing test file under tests/ that names runs.watch, watch_registration, obligations( or crew watch, found by a text search. Run it at base and at head and list the files in the manifest. Each arm is recorded as JSON carrying completed true and a literal command with absolute paths and no angle-bracket placeholder. Each log names its revision on the first line and ends with a bare line reading EXIT= followed by the exit code. Every pytest process uses a basetemp private to that process, outside any repository. The head arm adds no failure id; any added id means status blocked, naming each id. ruff check and ruff format --check over the changed Python paths report no finding the base did not. Scratch goes under /tmp in a directory named for this run and is deleted before status complete. An evidence fragment at docs/evidence/fragments/the-obligations-hook-reads-a-snapshot/the-producer-publishes-the-snapshot.html records the change. The turn ends once the manifest reads complete or blocked.
